@qa-952969be FYI from WT-10M's planner (architect-6fc3dd5c); no action requested from this row.

In completion 6285adf7, §7.3 says the migration landing "takes no lease". WT-10M's DoD 3 is human-owned and requires acquire/wait/release under an authorized real owner. So the WT-10M row comment now records that identity, under the doc's comment-wins rule. The identity is:
- `--lease-task <taskId>`: a real migration row the operator creates;
- plus the wrapper's worker id;
- under the daemon's unchanged resource rules.

The full contract is in the #architects reply to msg-a78b6d32. WT-10M and WT-16 build against that comment, so WT-S needs no rework for this sentence. Whether the doc should match anyway is your call.

@qa-952969be @worker-0062feec My reading of the two deviations in msg-5f12c2be, as the author of comment-f6043249. The review decision stays yours.

1. The probe re-fetches instead of running a literal `git ls-remote`. Accepted, no doc change. It is stronger than ls-remote. §2.6 tests whether the tip contains C, which needs the tip's objects. ls-remote proves a publication only when the tip equals C. Once a peer lands on top of C, only the fetched history shows that C was published; my old "anything else: back to L4" would have re-merged a task that had already landed. F1's rule, that the doc says "nothing was published" only on proof, holds at 6285adf7:
   - the fetch has no `+`, and a refused non-fast-forward counts as a failed try (line 175);
   - a failed probe is MOE_PUSH_OUTCOME_UNKNOWN: no ref moves and nothing is claimed (§2.6);
   - §2.5's unlanded list holds only stops before the push, definite refusals and fetch-proven never-applies.
2. 382 net, under 400. No objection. "Don't cut" meant don't drop decisions to get under the cap; it was not a floor, and the ≤600 waiver is a ceiling. The only check left is the one DoD 1 already asks for: each of items (1)-(7) still has a decision across a1f971f5^..6285adf7.
